==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|hendiaduo}}

===Etymology===
{{bor|en|ML.|-}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἕν}}, stem of {{m|grc|εἷς||one}} + {{m|grc|διά||through}} + {{m|grc|δύο||two}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/hɛnˈdaɪ.ədɪs/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-hendiadys.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{examples|examples=
* “[[safe and sound]]”
* “nice and warm” (for “nicely warm”)
* “sound and fury” (for “furious sound”)
* “come and get it” (for “come get it”)
}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} A [[figure of speech]] used for [[emphasis]], where two words joined by ''[[and]]'' are used to express a single complex idea.
#: {{cot|en|hendiatris}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Wilfred G. E. Watson|title=Classical Hebrew Poetry|year=1984|publisher=A&C Black|page=327|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ohN0CVPCR2AC&pg=PA327&dq=%22hendiadys%22&hl=en&cd=7&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22hendiadys%22&f=false|isbn=978-0-567-43416-6|passage=Some examples of '''hendiadys''' comprise two words in the bound state; others, two words in appositional '''hendiadys'''. It would seem that certain cases of appositional '''hendiadys''' are closely related to wordpairs (see WORD-PAIRS, BREAK-UP), though which way the development proceeded is far from certain.}}

